WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
aggio.com

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:45.360
An eye from another world.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.800
A smell detector investigating the path ahead.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.600
We don't often see a snail that way.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.280
And that's because we've only recently had the tiny lenses and electronic cameras that

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:08.880
we need to explore this miniature world.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.760
But when we meet its inhabitants face to face, we suddenly realize that their behavior can

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:20.800
be just as meaningful to us as the behavior of many animals more our own size.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.560
Look at this, for example.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.720
It's an earwig, yes.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.920
But it's also a female and a mother.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:39.760
And like so many mothers, she's guarding her young.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.960
These two ants are not quite sure whether they like one another.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:51.900
Stroking Antennae is the equivalent of a cautious chat over the garden fence.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:02:01.520
When big animals go courting, they show off and so do damselflies.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.040
Courtship signals for the male wolf spider are rather more frantic because if his female

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:13.840
doesn't understand why he's approaching her, she'll eat him.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.600
This ant is a farmer and these aphids, the cows, which it milks for a drink of honeydew

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.560
every day.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.440
Other ants are eternally on the march.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.680
Powerfully armed soldiers guard the flanks of their column as they travel, protecting

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:42.120
the workers who are carrying their helpless young.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:48.840
When it comes to craftsmanship, few can beat this wasp using mud to construct an elegant

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.520
jar in which to store her eggs.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.000
Mud is also used by termites.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.500
They build tower blocks that in proportion to their size are taller than New York skyscrapers.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:09.040
These two worlds, ours and theirs, influence one another to an extraordinary degree.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.600
If we and the rest of the backbone animals were to disappear overnight, the rest of the

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.800
world would get on pretty well.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:24.360
But if they were to disappear, the land's ecosystems would collapse.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.880
For the fact is, they were the pioneers, the first animals of any kind to colonize the

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.880
lands of the earth.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:58.000
To tell their story, we must go back to a time when the world was a very different place.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:05.960
Some 400 million years ago, the lands of planet earth were totally without life.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:13.280
They were bare naked rock, roasted by sun during the day, freezing cold at night, and

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:16.580
swept by terrible storms.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:23.040
But in the waters of the world, conditions were much more stable.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.840
Life had begun there some 2,000 million years earlier still.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.400
For a long time, it remained microscopic, but eventually larger animals appeared.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:42.880
Jellyfish and corals, starfish and snails, and animals with segmented bodies.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.080
All needed food.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.800
Many would have eaten unguarded eggs given the chance.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:55.720
And then around 400 million years ago, some enterprising creatures found it safer to lay

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.520
their eggs out at the sea, up on a beach.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:07.520
They still do.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:20.600
Every spring, on a few special nights along the Atlantic coast of North America, thousands

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:30.480
of horseshoe crabs emerge from the sea.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.560
And here, in the wet sand, they spawn.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.680
They may only stay for a few minutes or hours, but animals like these may well have been

00:05:39.680 --> 00:06:01.880
the first of any kind to leave the sea and venture onto land.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:08.040
Although these creatures spend virtually all their lives at sea, they can survive surprisingly

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.560
well on land.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.720
It's almost as if they were pre-adapted.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:21.240
They have shells, external skeletons, and that means that their legs are rigid and jointed.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:29.280
And at the back, they have a series of plates called book lungs, which extract oxygen from

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:36.040
seawater, but can also do the same thing if they're kept reasonably moist from the air.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:41.920
So creatures like this can, in fact, spend about a week on land.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:52.960
And it only requires minimal modifications to enable them to live up there permanently.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:59.160
It was difficult to abandon the sea altogether until the land became green, but eventually

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.800
it did.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.280
Several plants, algae and then mosses and liverworts, began to advance over the mud

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.520
and rock to clothe the earth.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:26.880
And into these first green tackles came animals looking for food.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.160
Some had armour, for that in the sea had protected them from their enemies.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:50.480
Now it would help them conserve moisture.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:58.800
They were the ancestors of today's millipedes.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.440
Small holes had developed along the underside of their bodies that led to internal tubes

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:11.400
with which they could absorb oxygen from the air.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:16.920
Their rigid, jointed legs, however, were largely unchanged and worked very well on land, even

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:25.240
without the support of water.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:30.080
Battering ram heads enabled them to bulldoze their way through the vegetation to collect

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:40.400
the rotting plants on which they fed.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.400
They grew big, increasing the number of segments in their bodies.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:51.840
Some had over 300, each with two pairs of legs.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:09:09.520
Some that didn't curl up reinforced their armour with plates along their backs.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:15.040
Frustations like shrimps came too.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:31.280
They were the ancestors of woodlice.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:37.000
So today there is a huge and varied population of animals living on the land with bodies

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.560
that are a little different from those of their ancestors who lived in the sea so long

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:45.880
ago.

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:49.120
And they are extraordinarily successful.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:55.440
Some are the most numerous of all land-living species, but we seldom see them.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:59.640
This pin will give you an idea of why.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.560
They're tiny.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.440
This minute little creature is a springtail.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:12.720
It's less than half a millimetre long, the size of a full stop.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:19.360
In one square metre of soil there may be over 10,000 of them.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:25.240
Drying out is a very real danger for them and some waterproof themselves regularly with

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:28.200
a droplet of special grooming fluid.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:37.880
You might even say that they have turned bathing into an art form.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:50.400
They even have two inflatable tubes that enable them to get to those hard to reach places.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:55.880
To help them get around through the leaf litter, these springtails, as their name suggests,

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:01.920
have a rather novel way of jumping.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:10.000
They have a tiny two-pronged lever beneath their abdomen.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.240
One small flick from it can catapult them six inches, some 15 centimetres, into the

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:21.760
air.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:29.920
That's the equivalent of a human being jumping over the Eiffel Tower.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:37.400
And if they happen to land upside down, well, they have a special way of writing themselves.

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:42.360
They use their grooming fluid dispenser to stick onto the ground so that they can pull

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:51.840
themselves back onto their feet.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:58.340
So the foundations were laid for the ecosystems that now flourish on earth and on which we

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:04.240
ourselves depend.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:10.140
It has to be said, however, that sometimes some of us regard a few of these pioneers

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:17.920
more as our enemies than our friends.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:24.800
Many of the mollusks in the sea develop shells to protect themselves from predators.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:31.140
But on land, those shells serve just as well to keep the occupant nice and moist.

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:36.800
So without any major change to their anatomy, mollusks were able to creep up out of the

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:43.360
water and graze in the forests of algae and mosses that were then spreading over the land.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:58.160
And given the right conditions, they still do.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:11.160
With rain and the coming of night, a secret army comes out of hiding.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.800
These are the conditions they like best.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:37.760
Dark and, best of all, wet.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:45.080
Hiding along a carpet of slime works just as well on land as it does underwater.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:55.520
And a rasping tongue scrapes algae off rocks wherever they are.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:14:00.740
In times of drought, snails may be unable to move around for months on end.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:10.000
So when conditions are right, they eagerly set off to find food.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.680
Their upper pair of tentacles carry those eyes with which they look around.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:28.900
The lower ones smell what's beneath.

00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:34.180
They breathe by means of a small pouch on the right-hand side of their body just within

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:47.480
the shell, which, because it's permanently moist, is able to absorb oxygen.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.200
This is what they're seeking.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.560
A succulent green leaf.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:15:03.440
No time to be lost.

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:08.200
Dawn will bring a change in conditions.

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:12.920
So they have to return to their shelters and clamp down their shells once more so that

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:21.720
they retain their moisture.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:26.920
The ancient forests were colonized by all kinds of plant eaters long before there were

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:31.960
any frogs or lizards, birds or insect-eating mammals.

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:38.760
But there were, nonetheless, hunters prowling through the vegetation.

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:42.000
This was one of the first.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:47.840
Holes very like it have been found in rocks that are 540 million years old.

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.280
This is a velvet worm.

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:57.360
It too has scarcely changed since it lived in the sea, and today it's only found in

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:00.960
wet, humid forests.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:07.760
It usually hunts at night, but infrared cameras can reveal it in action.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:13.920
Soft, stumpy legs enable it to move in total silence, and it finds its way with long, sensitive

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:16.440
feelers.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:20.560
It's a master of stealth.

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:32.960
This cricket has huge eyes, but it's difficult to see what's going on around it.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:37.440
Although the velvet worm has fangs, it will attack its prey when it finds it with a very

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:13.960
anxiously, the cricket probes around in the darkness with its long antennae.

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:19.640
The velvet worm will only know if it's found its prey when it touches it, so when it does,

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:23.840
it has to react immediately.

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:26.440
There!

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:34.720
And the cricket is trapped.

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:40.640
A slow-motion camera shows the remarkable way in which the velvet worm attacks.

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:53.920
Two nozzles beneath its feelers squirt twin streams of glue.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:18:01.540
The more the cricket struggles, the more it becomes entangled.

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:07.600
With the prey immobilized, the velvet worm reclaims its glue by eating it, and then it

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:19.480
starts on the cricket.

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:25.240
There were other hunters, too, in the ancient forests, relatives of the horseshoe crabs,

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:33.680
and they were even more formidable.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:36.620
This is a whip spider.

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:42.640
Like its ancestors, it has a hard external skeleton.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:48.320
Two of its limbs have been turned into highly mobile, sensitive feelers.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:53.120
It uses them to probe around delicately both in front and behind.

00:18:53.120 --> 00:19:02.560
Any prey within the foot of it will be immediately detected.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:13.320
It's extremely territorial, and it has no hesitation in attacking one of its own kind.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:18.520
The width of its claws is a good indicator of strength, and a smaller animal will quickly

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:21.400
back down.

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:44.640
But these two are equally matched, and they will fight.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:49.920
The loser retreats.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:54.600
But even whip spiders were not the most formidable hunters in these forests.

00:19:54.600 --> 00:20:03.900
There were others with an even more venomous weaponry.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:11.320
This centipede has powerful jaws, poison fangs, and is very, very fast.

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:13.760
It's a very good hunter.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:17.720
But it's only half as long as my little finger.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:25.800
There are centipedes in the world, however, that are as big as my forearm.

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:29.160
This is one of these alarming giants.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:34.880
It's over 13 inches, 35 centimeters long, and with the muscular strength of a small

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:36.160
snake.

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:42.560
And the poison in its black-tipped fangs is lethal.

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:53.600
It hunts in the dark, bat-haunted caves of Venezuela.

00:20:53.600 --> 00:21:01.000
Like the whip spider and the velvet worm, it uses its antennae to feel for its victims.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:05.400
The beetles that swarm on the rocky floor of the cave are of no interest to it.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:09.480
It's after bigger prey.

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:18.800
And it knows it can find that by climbing.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:22.920
Its many legs give it a secure hold on the vertical rocks.

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:36.840
It's heading for the ceiling.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:44.880
Now in the darkness, it can sense bats flying past it.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:51.080
Holding on with its hind legs, it reaches out into their flight path, and almost immediately,

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:58.200
it has one.

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:07.100
An injection of venom from its fangs kills the bat almost instantaneously.

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:19.720
It will take it an hour or so, but it will eat all the bats fresh.

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:25.040
So all these animals, having left the sea, solved the problems of moving around and breathing

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:27.920
air in their own differing ways.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:31.240
But there was another difficulty, mating.

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:36.840
In the sea, animals need only release their eggs and sperm and the water mix the two together.

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:44.040
On dry land, that couldn't happen, even for the most moisture loving of creatures.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:49.560
An individual's slug carries both male and female organs, but even then, that was of

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:50.560
no help.

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:54.480
Each had to both give and receive.

00:22:54.480 --> 00:23:01.160
Somehow or other, pairs of individuals had to get together, and the ways they have evolved

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:04.320
in which to do so are quite extraordinary.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:10.160
Indeed, some of them are almost beyond imagining.

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:14.560
The leopard slug, you might think, has the simplest of habits.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:19.500
Maybe, but not when it comes to mating.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:24.440
When an individual is looking for a partner, it gives its trail of slime a special taste

00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:26.560
that advertises the fact.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:33.860
Another, if it feels the same way, will detect the invitation and start to follow.

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:45.160
The pursuer, to confirm that it's there and ready to mate, gives the pursued a nibble.

00:23:45.160 --> 00:24:03.440
The leader heads upwards, an overhang is what's needed.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:07.160
The underside of a branch will do very nicely.

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:16.600
The two start to circle one another more and more closely until they entwine.

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:33.200
For an hour or so, they continue to wind themselves around one another.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:39.320
Then suddenly, the pair release their hold on the branch and start to slide downwards

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:48.600
on a rope of mucus.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:25:03.480
Now, in midair, they move to the next stage in their pairing.

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:17.880
Each averts its male organ from just behind its head.

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:28.840
These grow longer and longer.

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:54.800
Then they too begin to entwine.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:26:08.120
They fan out to form a translucent, flower-like globe.

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:15.560
And now, at last, sperm passes from one slug to the other.

00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:19.320
The transfer is complete.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:26.720
Each has been fertilized.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:41.800
Finally, their strange, poetic relationship comes to an end with a bump.

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:47.200
A millipede, unlike a slug, is either a male or a female.

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:53.280
In southern Africa, where there are many different species, both sexes spend the winter in hibernation,

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:57.840
curled up in the leaf litter or beneath the bark.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:02.400
As the temperatures rise with the coming of spring, they all unwind themselves and set

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:04.960
off to look for a mate.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:09.000
Finding one in the tangled undergrowth is not easy.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:18.680
But this male forest millipede knows that he can increase his chances if he heads upwards

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:25.200
into the trees.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:29.440
Leaving the safety of the undergrowth may seem a risky thing to do, but these millipedes

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:34.520
secrete a poison from pores in their armor, and their conspicuous red and black colors

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:37.680
warn predators to leave them alone.

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:44.160
They emerge in thousands.

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:49.240
Surprisingly perhaps, a male, when he does find a female, is not met with a friendly

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:50.240
greeting.

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:51.400
Quite the reverse.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:57.520
She coils up.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:01.200
This is her way of sorting out the men from the boys.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:07.720
Only the strongest and fittest male will have the strength to force her coils apart.

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:12.780
To help him do so, he has white suction pads on the bottom of his feet, which give him

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:15.000
a good grip.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:22.220
Eventually, she relaxes, and he lifts her up so he can extend two specially modified

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:30.560
legs with which he inseminates her.

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:36.200
Once inserted, these legs swell so that the partners become fastened together.

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:44.040
And that's important, because it will take him a couple of hours to transfer his sperm.

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:53.080
But there are lots of males around, and before long, another one turns up.

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:56.580
The new arrival checks out the pair with his antennae.

00:28:56.580 --> 00:29:05.080
If they're not tightly bound together, he may have a chance of taking over.

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:08.560
He pushes between them, levering them apart.

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:16.360
Gradually, he manages to unzip their legs.

00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:21.140
The first male's white mating legs are dragged out.

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:22.540
He's been defeated.

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:30.480
It will be the second, stronger male who fertilizes her eggs.

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:35.480
So mating on land isn't as random as it had been for so many in the sea.

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:37.700
Now it's selective.

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:42.280
But brute force isn't the only basis on which to select.

00:29:42.280 --> 00:29:47.640
A female's spring tail is bigger than a male, and she prefers a partner who can give her

00:29:47.640 --> 00:29:52.900
a sustained head-to-head push.

00:29:52.900 --> 00:30:12.640
Other males are eager to try their luck, but butting her sides won't get them anywhere.

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:31.760
She seems unimpressed by any of them, but one is determined to stay as her dance partner.

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:40.440
She simply can't get rid of him.

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:48.080
He confidently signals victory with a couple of fancy twirls.

00:30:48.080 --> 00:31:04.440
Then he deposits a droplet of sperm onto the leaf, and she graciously takes it on board.

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:09.320
One group of colonists were of particular importance, for they changed the nature of

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:15.760
the soil, and thus made it possible for new kinds of plants and animals to evolve.

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:19.760
They outweigh all other animals in any given area of the forest.

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:25.120
A single hectare may be home to eight million of them.

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:29.520
They spend nearly all their time below ground.

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:31.820
Worms.

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:37.480
They eat their way through the earth, extracting edible vegetable material and making it suitable

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:40.600
for plants.

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:58.080
And at night, they come to the surface and collect dead leaves.

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:07.960
They also take the opportunity to call on their neighbors, poking their heads into next-door

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:08.960
burrows.

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:09.960
They're looking for partners.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:16.080
Like slugs, they're hermaphrodite, each individual both male and female.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:19.160
They mate by lying alongside one another.

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:22.720
Two narrow grooves form between their two bodies.

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:28.560
These are the conduits that carry sperm from one partner to the other.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:35.480
Their bodies slowly pulse as sperm travels along the space between them.

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:40.880
But the process is a long one, and it may be three hours or so before they separate,

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:50.200
each carrying the other's sperm.

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:56.000
Like so many of the inhabitants of the undergrowth, earthworms can only live in a moist environment,

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:01.560
but they are found in soils of every continent except Antarctica.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:08.400
This small valley in southern Australia is home to one of the rarest and the most extraordinary

00:33:08.400 --> 00:33:09.400
of all earthworms.

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:19.200
And I know they're around because I can hear them.

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:24.480
Those gurgling noises, believe it or not, are being made by giant earthworms as they

00:33:24.480 --> 00:33:34.800
squelch along their water-filled burrows.

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:43.200
The vibrations of my footsteps are enough to stir them into activity.

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:48.080
They never come to the surface, but in places where there's been a small landslip, you can

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:57.600
sometimes find their burrows.

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:03.160
They are over an inch in diameter, and in them, if you're very lucky, you may occasionally

00:34:03.160 --> 00:34:06.760
find one of these.

00:34:06.760 --> 00:34:12.600
This is one of their egg cocoons, and it's enormous.

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:19.480
If I hold it up against the light, I can see the young worm inside wriggling.

00:34:19.480 --> 00:34:27.120
It'll take a year for this to develop, and when the young one finally does break free,

00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:32.040
it's already 20 centimetres long.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:34.140
Huge.

00:34:34.140 --> 00:34:41.520
It will take a further five years to reach full size and become this remarkable creature.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:45.920
So the question is, how long is a giant earthworm?

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:48.760
Well, it's not an easy question to answer.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:53.680
The fact of the matter is, they're rather delicate creatures, and they break.

00:34:53.680 --> 00:35:01.880
If I was so unfeeling as to try and stretch it, well, I guess it might stretch to a couple

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:08.320
of metres, almost six feet long.

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:09.320
How long do they live?

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:15.480
Well, some say up to 20 years, but we really don't know, and we certainly don't know how

00:35:15.480 --> 00:35:26.760
they manage to mate deep underground as they sculp their way through their lonely tunnels.

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:31.720
The land may have been a safe place for eggs when horseshoe crabs first laid theirs up

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:37.120
on the beach, but as new kinds of animals appeared, so it became increasingly important

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:40.040
for animals to protect their eggs.

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:46.280
Most creatures just hid them, but a few now actively defend them.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:51.640
The builder of this circular mud wall in the Central American rainforest is one.

00:35:51.640 --> 00:35:58.080
During the day, he conceals himself, but when night comes, he emerges to inspect his collection

00:35:58.080 --> 00:36:03.080
of eggs.

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:06.240
His body is smaller than a grain of wheat.

00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:12.080
He's a relative of the spiders, a harvester.

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:17.600
His eggs, up to 100 of them, are half-buried in the floor of his nest, and he regularly

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:25.800
inspects each one of them.

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:35.400
If it has a fungus on it, he carefully cleans it before putting it back into its moist bed.

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:42.000
He also continually repairs and improves his nest, for females will only call on those

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:47.520
who have well-built and well-kept ones.

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:50.960
Some males, however, follow a different policy.

00:36:50.960 --> 00:36:53.980
They don't bother to build a nest for themselves.

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:57.360
They try to take over an existing one.

00:36:57.360 --> 00:37:07.360
A nest holder has to leave sometimes to feed, and that gives an intruder his chance.

00:37:07.360 --> 00:37:18.920
But the owner is back almost immediately, and they fight, trying to bite one another

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:39.720
in the weakest point of their armour, the joints of the legs.

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:49.880
The intruder retreats, and the nest owner checks his eggs.

00:37:49.880 --> 00:37:56.160
No damage done.

00:37:56.160 --> 00:38:05.760
And then another, more welcome visitor arrives.

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:07.560
This is a female.

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:12.160
She's bigger than he is, and she's touring all the nests in the neighbourhood to choose

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:20.760
the one where her eggs will be best cared for.

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:25.320
She seems to approve of the standard of his housekeeping.

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:33.240
So now, face to face through the tangle of legs, she mates with him.

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:43.560
He has a rod with which he injects his sperm.

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:54.200
He withdraws, and she's been fertilised.

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:59.880
Half an hour later, she lowers her white tubular ovipositor, feeling for a suitable place for

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:04.160
her egg.

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:08.680
She thrusts the egg into the floor of the nest, and then covers it with a thin blanket

00:39:08.680 --> 00:39:28.520
of mud.

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:30.000
She leaves.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:34.560
He will now tend and guard the egg with the rest of his collection for the month that

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:39.840
it will take to hatch.

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:44.280
His nest is clearly one of the best in the neighbourhood, for throughout the night, a

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:47.200
succession of females call on him.

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:51.320
But not all have come to lay.

00:39:51.320 --> 00:39:54.840
Life is not that simple.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:40:00.840
This one starts, as usual, with a routine inspection, and then, without more ado, she

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:06.040
mates.

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:09.280
He waits for her to produce a new egg.

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:18.800
But nothing appears.

00:40:18.800 --> 00:40:30.280
He constantly checks the nest floor with his feelers, but there are no signs of a new egg.

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:33.480
And then she grabs one from his collection.

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:37.600
She wants to eat one.

00:40:37.600 --> 00:40:39.880
She grabs again.

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:49.280
He bites her leg joints and tries to pull her away.

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:57.060
She's had enough, and he has rescued his egg.

00:40:57.060 --> 00:41:04.840
He checks it over, cleans it with great care, and then takes it away to rebury it.

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:26.600
A month after the eggs were laid, his young begin to emerge.

00:41:26.600 --> 00:41:32.480
The skins from which they hatched provide them with their first meal.

00:41:32.480 --> 00:41:40.840
He will now guard his young for a couple of days until they leave the nest.

00:41:40.840 --> 00:41:45.200
Excellently adapted, though harvestsmen are to a life on land, they cannot survive for

00:41:45.200 --> 00:41:51.520
very long away from this damp undergrowth.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:56.760
In fact, most of the direct descendants of those early colonists that came from the sea

00:41:56.760 --> 00:42:08.680
are still trapped in a world of moisture.

00:42:08.680 --> 00:42:19.160
Those with no external skeletons are always in imminent danger of death by drought.

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:25.720
Even those with exoskeletons are not safe, for most have armour that is not totally watertight

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:40.840
and will eventually dry out and die if they leave the dank shelter of the undergrowth.

00:42:40.840 --> 00:42:46.200
But beyond the reach of the forests, in the centre of continents where little or no rain

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:58.080
falls, there is a very different territory, empty and hostile.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:02.440
Here there is little shelter from the scorching sun.

00:43:02.440 --> 00:43:08.680
Temperatures rise above 70 degrees centigrade, and there may be no rain whatsoever for years

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:18.520
on end.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:24.280
Deserts like this one in the south-west of the United States represented the ultimate

00:43:24.280 --> 00:43:30.320
challenge for those ancient creatures whose ancestors first left the sea.

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:34.320
Here there is virtually no water at all.

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:42.080
And yet, those early creatures, the very first to walk on land, reached even here.

00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:45.800
And they're still around.

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:52.840
In order to survive the ferocious heat of the day, they take refuge in little burrows

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:57.560
like this, which go quite a long way down into the ground.

00:43:57.560 --> 00:44:19.840
And I can use this special optical probe to see whether anyone's at home.

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:20.840
And there it is.

00:44:20.840 --> 00:44:24.040
It's a scorpion.

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:30.520
They won't come out for the rest of the day, but at night, when it gets cool, scorpions

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:34.360
all over the desert will be emerging.

00:44:34.360 --> 00:44:40.800
And then we have a very special way of finding them.

00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:44.940
In ultraviolet light, scorpions are magically transformed.

00:44:44.940 --> 00:44:48.520
They glow with fluorescence.

00:44:48.520 --> 00:44:53.960
So with an ultraviolet torch, you can get a better idea of just how abundant scorpions

00:44:53.960 --> 00:45:01.440
actually are, even in this arid wilderness.

00:45:01.440 --> 00:45:09.960
That's because they have managed to develop external skeletons that are virtually watertight.

00:45:09.960 --> 00:45:16.440
They also have powerful stings and pincers, so getting together to mate could be dangerous.

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:24.680
A male looking for a female must be careful.

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:32.320
She is powerful enough to kill and eat him.

00:45:32.320 --> 00:45:43.080
So he begins to dance.

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:55.120
Is she impressed?

00:45:55.120 --> 00:46:15.700
Apparently so, and his solo becomes a pas de deux.

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:41.160
But stings are still held high, ready to strike.

00:46:41.160 --> 00:46:47.480
She tries to sting him.

00:46:47.480 --> 00:46:57.140
His response is to give her a dose of her own medicine with a quick jab.

00:46:57.140 --> 00:47:03.960
But it's so slight, it merely makes her a little drowsy.

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:06.580
At last, she seems more amenable.

00:47:06.580 --> 00:47:10.760
He pulls her to a part of the dance ground that is smoother level.

00:47:10.760 --> 00:47:16.880
He has extruded a small packet of sperm on a stalk glued to the ground.

00:47:16.880 --> 00:47:22.320
He maneuvers her so that as she dances, she goes over the stalk and takes the sperm packet

00:47:22.320 --> 00:47:25.320
up into her body.

00:47:25.320 --> 00:47:34.160
The nuptial dance is over.

00:47:34.160 --> 00:47:39.420
Her fertilized eggs stay within a special chamber in her body for more than a year while

00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:42.920
they slowly develop.

00:47:42.920 --> 00:47:50.360
And then in her burrow deep underground, she gives birth.

00:47:50.360 --> 00:47:53.220
She has produced up to 50 young ones.

00:47:53.220 --> 00:47:58.240
They cling tightly to her back for a few weeks after birth, each sustained by a small blob

00:47:58.240 --> 00:48:03.880
of yolk in its stomach.

00:48:03.880 --> 00:48:15.640
And then at last, they're all ready to venture into the open desert for themselves.

00:48:15.640 --> 00:48:22.380
By colonizing this the most hostile of environments, the first animals to walk on land finally

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:25.160
broke their link with open water.

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:32.360
And they did that about 300 million years ago, at a time when the animals with backbones,

00:48:32.360 --> 00:48:56.240
including our own ancestors, were still swimming in the seas.

00:48:56.240 --> 00:49:00.600
For the last 10 minutes of each episode of Life in the Undergrowth, I'm going to take

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:10.440
you behind the scenes to look at some of the problems we faced when making the programs.

00:49:10.440 --> 00:49:16.200
During the two years it took to make the whole series, we went to some surprising places.

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:18.840
Guten Morgen!

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:24.560
We teamed up with scientists who were researching into the lives of the animals we wanted to

00:49:24.560 --> 00:49:25.560
show.

00:49:25.560 --> 00:49:29.880
And we used some of the very latest technical gear to capture some really extraordinary

00:49:29.880 --> 00:49:31.640
behavior.

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:38.880
Now, in Fly on the Wall, you can join me and find out just what it takes to bring the world

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:54.520
of the undergrowth into your living room.

00:49:54.520 --> 00:49:59.240
In the next episode, we look into the many differing ways in which some of the inhabitants

00:49:59.240 --> 00:50:12.960
of the undergrowth mastered the power of flight.

00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:18.240
Among the first to do so over 300 million years ago were the mayflies.

00:50:18.240 --> 00:50:22.960
We'd been told that on this river in Hungary in early summer, vast numbers of them take

00:50:22.960 --> 00:50:28.920
to the air in the simultaneous mass emergence.

00:50:28.920 --> 00:50:31.760
The event sounded so dramatic.

00:50:31.760 --> 00:50:36.560
Producer Mike Salisbury and cameraman Martin Dawn are driving all the way from England

00:50:36.560 --> 00:50:40.520
to film it.

00:50:40.520 --> 00:50:42.400
But there's a problem.

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:50.120
It's nearly the end of May and there's not a mayfly to be seen.

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:57.200
The man who advised us to come to this river at this particular time is Joseph St. Péter.

00:50:57.200 --> 00:51:01.240
He's a biologist who studied the mayflies for three years.

00:51:01.240 --> 00:51:06.200
If anyone knows about mayflies, it ought to be him.

00:51:06.200 --> 00:51:11.920
As the team arrive, they're anxious to find out what's gone wrong.

00:51:11.920 --> 00:51:13.600
Why do you think they're late?

00:51:13.600 --> 00:51:15.680
By this time last year, you said they were...

00:51:15.680 --> 00:51:20.080
Yeah, last year and the year before they were here.

00:51:20.080 --> 00:51:26.560
But we had very rainy May and the nights were really cold.

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:31.280
One of the most important factors for mayfly hatching is that the water needs to have a

00:51:31.280 --> 00:51:32.280
certain temperature.

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:35.280
It's a waiting game.

00:51:35.280 --> 00:51:37.280
Yeah, exactly.

00:51:37.280 --> 00:51:42.440
And a fingers crossed.

00:51:42.440 --> 00:51:46.080
Mayflies spend the first part of their lives under water as larvae.

00:51:46.080 --> 00:51:50.860
It's not until the water reaches a critical 18 degrees centigrade in the early summer

00:51:50.860 --> 00:51:56.100
that they shed their larval skins and emerge as winged adults.

00:51:56.100 --> 00:51:59.720
So mayflies like it sunny, warm and still.

00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:02.720
And that's the problem.

00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:03.720
Ah.

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:06.720
End of day one.

00:52:06.720 --> 00:52:11.880
No, this is end of the day.

00:52:11.880 --> 00:52:12.880
Just leave.

00:52:12.880 --> 00:52:15.880
Well, hope for tomorrow.

00:52:15.880 --> 00:52:18.880
That's it.

00:52:18.880 --> 00:52:22.840
We'll be fine.

00:52:22.840 --> 00:52:31.320
I have every confidence in the planning department.

00:52:31.320 --> 00:52:42.760
It rained day after day.

00:52:42.760 --> 00:52:46.080
Day seven and it's stopped.

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:49.600
But the wind and the rain have sent the water temperature plummeting.

00:52:49.600 --> 00:52:55.080
Any possibility of a hatch has now been set back two or three more days at least.

00:52:55.080 --> 00:52:59.720
Time is running out.

00:52:59.720 --> 00:53:07.440
Back in sunny England, we're having rather more success.

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:11.160
This is the very latest in slow motion video cameras.

00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:16.320
It was developed for scientific research and we are using it to reveal just how insects

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:19.560
beat their wings in flight.

00:53:19.560 --> 00:53:23.160
Just like this hoverfly.

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:27.400
One of the huge problems in natural history of filmmaking, how do you anticipate what

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:31.220
the creature you're trying to film is going to do?

00:53:31.220 --> 00:53:35.760
So often in the old days when you press the button, you just missed that particular moment

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:37.840
because you didn't know it was going to do it.

00:53:37.840 --> 00:53:43.240
But this is recording all the time so that when you press the button, it has already

00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:49.060
recorded and will keep what happened before you press the button.

00:53:49.060 --> 00:53:54.640
And not only does it enable us to record moments we missed in the past, it can also show them

00:53:54.640 --> 00:53:58.800
to us in glorious slow motion.

00:53:58.800 --> 00:54:03.640
It can see things that no human eye has ever, ever seen before.

00:54:03.640 --> 00:54:04.800
Ever.

00:54:04.800 --> 00:54:12.720
It slows things down 4,000 times if you wanted it to so that you can see the exact way in

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:18.480
which an insect wing might move.

00:54:18.480 --> 00:54:24.160
Once you can record the actions of tiny creatures with this degree of clarity and detail, you

00:54:24.160 --> 00:54:27.200
can explore a whole new world of aviation.

00:54:27.200 --> 00:54:31.440
A world of folding wings and beating flight sensors.

00:54:31.440 --> 00:54:36.440
It's not surprising that images like these are capturing the imagination of aeronautical

00:54:36.440 --> 00:54:39.440
engineers.

00:54:39.440 --> 00:54:46.000
That's beautiful.

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:52.320
That's wonderful.

00:54:52.320 --> 00:54:57.600
I'd heard that conditions were now better in Hungary, but as I arrived to do my piece

00:54:57.600 --> 00:55:02.560
for the opening of the programme, the hatch still hasn't started.

00:55:02.560 --> 00:55:07.440
Now we've only got one more day before we have to be in Switzerland to film the mating

00:55:07.440 --> 00:55:08.880
of wood ants.

00:55:08.880 --> 00:55:09.880
It's now or never.

00:55:09.880 --> 00:55:12.880
I mean, what's the chance for tomorrow?

00:55:12.880 --> 00:55:14.360
What, tomorrow?

00:55:14.360 --> 00:55:19.960
Well, you never know.

00:55:19.960 --> 00:55:26.600
I was beginning to wonder whether there really were any mayflies in this river after all.

00:55:26.600 --> 00:55:32.600
But Joseph has to predict not only when the emergence would happen, but where.

00:55:32.600 --> 00:55:39.180
He finds a sign, small but significant, the freshly discarded skin of a mayfly larva.

00:55:39.180 --> 00:55:42.400
This could be the prelude to a swarm.

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:49.400
And what's more, it gives Joseph a clue as to where it's most likely to happen.

00:55:49.400 --> 00:55:52.760
Next evening, the conditions look perfect.

00:55:52.760 --> 00:55:57.040
Sunny, warm and still.

00:55:57.040 --> 00:56:06.160
All eyes are on Joseph.

00:56:06.160 --> 00:56:10.520
We have a living proof right now that mayflies are still in this river.

00:56:10.520 --> 00:56:13.520
It's just right here.

00:56:13.520 --> 00:56:18.000
I'm very pleased.

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:22.920
Only one, but one is better than nothing and it means the rest will come.

00:56:22.920 --> 00:56:26.720
Well according to Joseph, anyway.

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:30.200
Thank you.

00:56:30.200 --> 00:56:31.960
Joseph was right.

00:56:31.960 --> 00:56:38.280
Soon one was followed by others and then hundreds, then thousands and hundreds of thousands

00:56:38.280 --> 00:56:40.920
until the spectacle was dazzling.

00:56:40.920 --> 00:56:45.880
Joseph had not only brought us to the right place at the right time, well, almost the

00:56:45.880 --> 00:56:54.480
right time, but also to the most impressive hatch he'd ever seen.

00:56:54.480 --> 00:57:00.680
With just half an hour left of golden sunlight, all that remained was for me to do my piece

00:57:00.680 --> 00:57:03.400
and the sequence would be in the can.

00:57:03.400 --> 00:57:07.340
The powers of flight of the mayfly are not great.

00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:14.120
Just two pairs of rigid, unfoldable wings moving in a rather uncoordinated way.

00:57:14.120 --> 00:57:40.560
But that was the kind of flight used by the first creatures ever to fly on earth.
